\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Plan:
\fs48 \
Phase 4: Decentralized Failure Detection (Gossip Protocol)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Great work finishing Phase 3 \'97 you now have a system that replicates data to N=3 nodes and enforces R=2/W=2 quorums. The one gap: nodes only discover a peer is dead by 
\f2\i trying to talk to it and timing out
\f1\i0 , on every single request. Phase 4 fixes this by giving every node a continuously-updated, independent opinion of "who's alive" \'97 built entirely through peer-to-peer gossip, with no central coordinator.\
By the end of Phase 4, killing a node will cause the rest of the cluster to notice within a few seconds (not per-request timeouts), and a killed node coming back up will be correctly re-recognized as alive.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Step 0: Concepts you need before writing code\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Why not just ping everyone from everyone, all the time?
\f1\b0  That's O(N\'b2) connections and doesn't scale. Gossip solves this by having each node talk to only a 
\f2\i few random peers
\f1\i0  per round; information still spreads to the whole cluster exponentially fast (with 6 nodes, ~3 rounds is enough), and it degrades gracefully \'97 no single node is a bottleneck or single point of failure for failure detection itself. (Real systems like Cassandra use exactly this "scuttlebutt"-style approach; SWIM adds indirect ping/ack probing on top, which we're deliberately skipping for simplicity.)\

\f0\b Heartbeat counters, not wall-clock timestamps.
\f1\b0  Each node maintains its own counter that only 
\f2\i it
\f1\i0  increments. When gossiping, a node never says "I saw X alive at 3:45:02pm" (that requires synchronized clocks across machines \'97 exactly the problem discussed in DDIA Ch. 8's "Unreliable Clocks"). Instead it says "the last heartbeat counter value I've seen for X is 47." Peers just propagate the 
\f2\i highest counter value they've seen
\f1\i0  \'97 a logical clock, not a physical one.\

\f0\b Two different kinds of "time" are involved, and this trips people up:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The heartbeat 
\f2\i counter
\f1\i0  is logical \'97 never compare it across machines' clocks.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The 
\f2\i timeout
\f1\i0  decision ("I haven't heard anything new about X in 4 seconds, so I'll call it dead") uses each node's own local monotonic clock, measuring elapsed time 
\f2\i since that node itself last observed new information
\f1\i0 . Nobody ever compares their local clock to another machine's clock. This sidesteps clock-skew problems entirely.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 The restart problem (a real gotcha you will hit in testing).
\f1\b0  If node X crashes and restarts, its heartbeat counter resets to 0. But every other node in the cluster still remembers X's 
\f2\i last
\f1\i0  counter value before it died (say, 9000). If your merge logic is "bigger counter wins," X's fresh heartbeat of 1 will be 
\f2\i ignored forever
\f1\i0  because 1 < 9000 \'97 X will appear dead forever even though it's back up! The fix: each node also tags its heartbeats with a 
\f0\b start epoch
\f1\b0  (its own process-start timestamp, in milliseconds). When merging, compare 
\f3\fs26 (start_epoch, heartbeat)
\f1\fs24  as a pair: a 
\f2\i higher start_epoch always wins outright
\f1\i0 , regardless of heartbeat value, because a higher epoch means "this is a newer incarnation of this process." Only if epochs are equal do you compare heartbeat counters. This is a simplified version of what SWIM calls "incarnation numbers."\

\f0\b Gossiping "ALIVE" news vs. discovering "DEAD" news are fundamentally different.
\f1\b0  Good news (a node's heartbeat count went up) needs to 
\f2\i spread
\f1\i0  via gossip \'97 it takes a few rounds to reach everyone. Bad news (a node stopped sending new heartbeats) does 
\f2\i not
\f1\i0  need to spread \'97 every node just independently notices "I haven't heard anything new about X in a while" using its own local clock. This means every alive node discovers a failure at roughly the same time, completely independently, without needing to wait for anyone to "tell" them.\

\f0\b Design decisions for Phase 4 (keep it simple on purpose):
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Full membership table gossiped every round (fine for 6 nodes; wouldn't scale to thousands, where you'd send deltas/digests instead).\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Status (ALIVE/SUSPECT/DEAD) is 
\f2\i never
\f1\i0  gossiped directly \'97 only raw 
\f3\fs26 (address, start_epoch, heartbeat)
\f1\fs24  tuples are exchanged. Status is always locally derived from elapsed time. This avoids messy "my peer told me X is dead but X's counter just went up" contradictions.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Fixed timeout thresholds, not adaptive/probabilistic detectors (Cassandra actually uses a Phi Accrual failure detector for this \'97 a good stretch goal later, mentioned in DDIA Ch. 8).\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Cluster membership is still static (
\f3\fs26 cluster.conf
\f1\fs24 ) \'97 a node marked DEAD is 
\f2\i not
\f1\i0  removed from the hash ring or preference lists. We only use the DEAD/ALIVE information to skip wasting time on doomed RPCs; the ring itself doesn't change yet. (True dynamic membership + hinted handoff is a good stretch goal, noted at the end.)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

