\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 A real C++ gotcha worth understanding, not skipping:
\f1\b0  the constructor needs to build 
\f3\fs26 membership_
\f1\fs24  using 
\f3\fs26 self_address
\f1\fs24  (the constructor parameter) 
\f2\i before
\f1\i0  that parameter gets 
\f3\fs26 std::move
\f1\fs24 'd into 
\f3\fs26 self_address_
\f1\fs24  (the member). Class members are initialized in 
\f0\b declaration order
\f1\b0 , regardless of the order you write them in the initializer list. So 
\f3\fs26 membership_
\f1\fs24  must be 
\f2\i declared
\f1\i0  before 
\f3\fs26 self_address_
